# <%= it.scope %>/runtime

For micro-frontends: the code that loads remotes from a registry. Everything in
[`@mfeflow/runtime`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/@mfeflow/runtime), plus the registry addresses of this company
and two helpers that use them. `/providers`, `/beacon`, `/plugins/report` and `/plugins/entry-retry` are
re-exported too. `@module-federation/enhanced` and `react` are peer dependencies. Leave this package out of a repo
whose apps do not run a shell.

## What to fill in

`src/defaults.ts` holds `registryUrls`: where the registry index is served in `dev`, `staging` and `prod`, primary
first. They are empty, so `loadRegistry` rejects until you fill them in.

`registryOptions(env, overrides?)` returns the options for `loadRegistryIndex`, allowing the origins of those
addresses. `loadRegistry(env, overrides?)` reads the index from them.

The runtime also reads `registryUrls` from the shell's runtime config, which is how a deployment changes the
address without a new build. Use `loadRegistry` where you want the address fixed in code.

```ts
import { loadRegistry } from '<%= it.scope %>/runtime';

const index = await loadRegistry('prod');
```

## Settings only, no state

`@mfeflow/runtime` is a Module Federation singleton, shared by package name. `<%= it.scope %>/runtime` is not in
that list, so every remote gets its own copy of this package while the one underneath stays shared. That is fine
while this package holds only settings and functions that build things. Keep it that way: no loaded index, cache or
flag at module level here. State belongs in `@mfeflow/runtime` or in the shell.

## In an app

An app that imports `<%= it.scope %>/runtime` must also list `@mfeflow/runtime` in its own `package.json` (the shell and every
remote). The preset shares `@mfeflow/runtime` only when the app can resolve it from its own folder, and pnpm does not
make a dependency of a dependency visible there. Without it the preset cannot see the package, leaves it out of the shared list, and each remote gets its own copy of the loader.
